{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786439999,"nonce":"F11C1P7v+JppZd8CEWouCDMsqtw=","title":"The UI has no login screen: an unauthenticated visitor sees skeleton rows forever","message":"**What is wrong.** The SolidJS app has no login route and no login form. `web/src/routes/` contains `alerts`, `alert-detail`, `groups`, `group-detail` and `settings` — nothing else. `auth/login` appears anywhere under `web/src` **only inside `api/generated/schema.d.ts`**; no component calls it.\n\n**What a visitor sees.** `/` redirects to `/alerts`, which immediately issues API calls that all answer **401**. The page renders **skeleton loading rows indefinitely** behind a badge reading *'Stale, retry in 2s'*. There is no error, no explanation, and no control anywhere that leads to signing in. `web/src/api/client.ts:86` maps 401 to the code `unauthenticated`, and nothing acts on it — there is no redirect, no interstitial, no message.\n\nTo use the app at all you must obtain the `oto_session` cookie out of band. The cookie is `HttpOnly`, so a user cannot set it from devtools without issuing the login request themselves.\n\n**Why it matters.** The server side is complete and correct — `POST /api/v1/auth/login` works, sets a hardened cookie (`HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax`), and the whole authenticated app works well once the cookie exists. So this is not a deep defect; it is a missing door on a finished building. But its effect is total: **on a fresh install nobody can use the UI**, and the failure presents as 'the product is broken and silent' rather than 'you need to sign in', which is the worst available reading.\n\nIt also makes the first sentence of issue 4892848's acceptance criteria — *'login, alert list, alert detail, ack, snooze, settings and the sources screen all work'* — unmeetable, and it means `internal/identity`'s login surface (which now has thorough tests) has no consumer in the product.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/App.tsx` (the route table), `web/src/routes/` (no login route), `web/src/api/client.ts:86` (401 mapped and then ignored), `internal/identity/api` (the working server side).\n\n**Done when.** An unauthenticated visitor is sent to a login screen rather than an infinite skeleton; the form posts to `/api/v1/auth/login` and lands the user on the alert list; a 401 from any later request returns them to it rather than showing a permanent loading state; and there is a way to sign out. Note the rate limiter on login is real and tested, so the form should render its 429 sensibly.\n\nFound by running the UI against a real backend for issue 4892848.","files":null}]}